,"code":"edgeCount - vertexCount + c"},{"type":"text","text":" cycles for c components. Roots are taken in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order and edges in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"type":"text","text":" order; a vertex’s tree edge is the one that discovered it. Self-loops and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"parallel edges are supported: a loop is a cycle of its own, and a second copy of an edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"closes a cycle of length 2."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleBasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/Walks\/documentation\/Walks\/Cycle","text":"Cycle","preciseIdentifier":"s:5Walks5CycleV"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex","text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Edges-swift.associatedtype","text":"Edges","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Index","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa"},{"kind":"text","text":">]"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every cycle in the basis has exactly one edge outside the forest, so the cycles are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"independent, and every cycle of the graph is a sum of them (mod 2). Each starts at its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least vertex in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" order and leaves it through the lesser of its two edges there.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"O(n + m) plus the total length of the cycles.","type":"text"}]}]}],"references":{"doc://Walks/documentation/Walks/Cycle":{"fragments":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Cycle","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edge","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":"> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"where","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SH","text":"Hashable","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edge","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SH","text":"Hashable","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"title":"Cycle","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/walks\/cycle","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A cycle: a closed path, at least one edge long, listed without repeating its start (NetworkX’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"simple_cycles"},{"type":"text","text":", petgraph). Vertex i goes to vertex (i + 1) mod "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"count"},{"type":"text","text":" through edge i, and no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex or edge position appears twice; a self-loop is a cycle of length 1, two parallel edges"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one of length 2. It is the witness cycle detection returns."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Walks\/documentation\/Walks\/Cycle"},"doc://Cycles/documentation/Cycles/GraphProtocols/Graph/cycleBasis()":{"role":"